From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ole Algoritme <olealgoritme@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 16:27:47 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 11/11] nvk, nvk/rm: compression for images outside dedicated
 allocations on GB20x

NVK compresses an image only when it has a dedicated allocation, and maps
it through the memory's own VA.  Real applications sub-allocate:
vkd3d-proton places every D3D12 resource in a heap, and native engines
bind their render targets into large allocations, so on RM every render
target was mapped with the uncompressed generic kind (0x6).

With NVK_RM_COMPRESS_ALL=1 (default off while it is measured):

- nvkmd: new NVKMD_MEM_COMPRESSIBLE memory flag and
  nvkmd_info::has_compressible_mem.  NVK asks for it on device-local
  memory that is neither host-visible nor shared nor imported, and an
  image that can be compressed is bound through its own VA with its
  compressed PTE kind, and marked compressed, when the backend kept the
  flag on the memory.

- RM backend: such memory is allocated the way dedicated image memory
  already is (block linear, 32 bpp, COMPR_ANY).  GB20x keeps compression
  state per physical page (GMMU v3 PTEs carry no comptag line), so there
  is no comptag pool to exhaust; RM still has to see a compressible
  allocation, as it maps a compressible kind over memory allocated
  without compression with the uncompressed kind.  The memory's own VA
  keeps RM's compressible kind (buffers, linear images, ZCULL), and an
  uncompressed block-linear image VA bound to compressible memory is
  given the compressible kind too, so that nothing accesses compressible
  pages uncompressed.  A refusal falls back to uncompressed memory.

Statistics as they grow and at device destruction: compressible memories
and their size, refusals, compressed image binds, upgraded binds.
